    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>As labour secretary after the 1943 coup and president from 1946, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-peron">Juan Perón</a> built his movement on the trade unions; real wages rose by 25% between 1943 and 1948, and workers won paid holidays, sick leave and a thirteenth-month bonus.</li><li>Its place on the spectrum is disputed: many scholars call it left-wing populism or a national, non-Marxist socialism, others a paternalistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a> or a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin American</a> fascism, a label most scholars reject.</li><li>After Perón its currents ranged from the revolutionary Montoneros and the orthodox right to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-menem">Carlos Menem</a>’s free-market reforms of 1989–1999 and the centre-left Kirchnerism that followed.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Perón said of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a>, whose Three Worlds Theory was seen as following his own Third Position, “That mischievous little Chinese is stealing my ideas”; Mao is reported to have asked young Argentine communists, “Why aren’t you Peronists?”</p></aside>
    </section>

